Assignment 1: Terrain Generation
This is a straight forward terrain generation program, that starts by creating the 21x81 2d array. It then generates two paths, one going north to south, one going east to west. Next, it places the two 2x2 buildings adjacent to a randomly selected set of paths. After that it will find 11 empty cells and seed them with the different terrain types:
    4 Clearings
    4 Tall Grass
    1 Water
    1 Tree
    1 Mountain
It then repeatedly grows these seeds by allowing them to expand into nearby empty cells, with a priority listing found in generation.c. This repeats until now empty cells are found, or until 50 iterations, after which it will fill any empty cells with trees. It then outputs the entire array.

Assignment 2: Map to Map Traversal
This is the next level of the terrain generation. You start in the middle of a world on a grid of 401x401 maps, each with the generation created in assignment 1, with slight adjustments, such as the two buildings appearing at a rate of 1 / D, where D is the Manhattan Distance from Map (0,0). the gates all line up with each adjacent map. The controls are as follow:
    n or N: Move to the map to the North
    s or S: Move to the map to the South
    e or E: Move to the map to the East
    w or W: Move to the map to the West
    q or Q: Quit
    .     : enter "Dev" mode, currently only allows flight to any map
    f_x*y : "f" can be upper or lowercase, the "_" must be and empty space (' '), and the * can be anycharacter, "x" and "y" can be any integer[-200, 200] i.e.: "f -167 92" would be a valid command assuming you've been to map (-167, 97) or have dev mode turned on
The inputs only take the first letter(except f), so if the input is "No way!" you will go north, and the rest of the characters will be ignored. At the moment you must press the return key to give the inputs to the game.

Assignment 3: Distance From Player
This assignment simulates the time it would take for Hiker NPCs and Rival NPCs to get to the player character using a dijkstra algorithm to find the shortest path. The player location is randomly generated to a valid position when the map is loaded, and the dijkstra algorithm then uses this location to find the distance from this tile to everyother tile in the map for both Hiker and Rivals using their respective time per tile type:
Hiker: {%(on border), ~, ^} = INF, {M, C} = 50, {%(internal), :} = 15, {., #} = 10
Rival: {%, ~ ^} = INF, {M, C} = 50, {:} = 15, {., #} = 10
After the map is generated and displayed, the program then displays the hiker distance map with a "@@" showing the tile that represents the player location, then followed by the rival distance map in the same format

Assignment 4: Player and NPCs
This assignemt adds player and NPC movement to the game. There is a heap based queue to determine movement order, and the character at the top of the queue is popped out, and makes it's move which adds to its nextTurn counter, and is then reinserted into the heap. On the players turn, the player will be prompted for their input with the following controls:
    w or W: Move up
    s or S: Move down
    a or A: Move left
    d or D: Move right
    ctrl + C: End the program
If the player tries to make a move that is not allowed their nextTurn count will increment, for example trying to move into the wall or another character. The default number of NPC is 20, but you may also put in an n number of NPCs with "./main --numtrainers n"

